--TEST--
A file part's two consumers are the two shapes and neither is the other: `content()` is a walk that no `array` binding accepts, and `readAll()` is one value that no `foreach` drives — `rule:http-server/a-part-is-consumed-in-one-of-three-ways`, `rule:iteration/foreach-subjects`, spec § 15
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The pair of edges that says § 3's split is real. `content()` answers a walk
// whose next chunk does not exist yet -- there is no snapshot behind it, so
// binding it as an `array<bytes>` would be asking for the whole upload under
// the name of the member that exists precisely to avoid holding one.
// `readAll()` is the other side: it answers the part, not a sequence of it, so
// a `foreach` over it has nothing to drive.
//
// Both refusals are what makes the choice between the two members a decision a
// program states rather than one it discovers at a memory limit.
foreach (Core\Request::files() as Core\Request\Part $part) {
    array<bytes> $chunks = $part->content();
    foreach ($part->readAll() as tainted bytes $chunk) {
    }
}
--EXPECT--

